          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#the_lagrangian The Lagrangian
          %p (…)
          %h3#boundary_conditions Boundary conditions
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+conditions"} boundary conditions
            of the BLG model, leading to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+intersection"} brane intersection
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-wave"} M-wave
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MO9-brane"} MO9-brane
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#ChuSmith09"}> Chu-Smith 09
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BPST09"} BPST 09
          %h3#3AlgebraStructure
            The “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            \-algebra” structure
          %p
            The BLG model Lagrangian involves a trilinear operation on the scalar fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mn 3
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-,-,-] : V^{\otimes 3} \to V
                  \\,,
          %p
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane+3-algebra"} M2-brane 3-algebra
          %p
            Moreover, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            of the Lagrangian hinges on the fact that this map satisfies a condition that has some similarity to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+identity"} Jacobi identity
            for the binary operation on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
          %p
            Therefore, superficially, it looks like this might be the trinary bracket on an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
            structure on the space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            On the one hand, indeed, by the discussion at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            , the M2-brane is charged under a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle 3-bundle with connection
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            is controlled by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+n-algebra"} Lie 3-algebras
            in direct analogy to how the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            is controled by
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbes"} gerbes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundles"} principal 2-bundles
            and their
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            by ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
          %p
            Motivated by attempts in
            %a{:href => "#BasuHarvey04"} Basu-Harvey 04
            to generalize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nahm%27s+equations"} Nahm's equations
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+funnels"} fuzzy funnels
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D2-D4-brane+intersections"} D2-D4-brane intersections
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-M5-brane+intersections"} M2-M5-brane intersections
            %a{:href => "#BaggerLambert06"} Bagger-Lambert 06
            introduced the algebraic structure that came to be known the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane+3-algebra"} M2-brane 3-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Gustavsson07"} Gustavsson 07
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-Lie+algebra"} 3-Lie algebra
          %p
            Beware that the Bagger-Lambert “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane+3-algebra"}> M2-brane 3-algebra
            ” is
            %em not- a
            = succeed "_" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+n-algebra"} Lie 3-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
            structure on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            concentrated in the lowest three degrees.
          %p
            The reason is that for the notion of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
            (as discussed there) it is crucial that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "-graded)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-proposal," do
              %a{:href => "#BaggerLamber"} BaggerLambert
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is all concentrated in a single degree (is regarded as ungraded). One immediately finds that in this case the
            = succeed "-respect" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-,-]
            for the grading would imply that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is taken to be in degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "-algebra." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
          %p
            Notice that the
            = succeed "-grading" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "-grading)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            interpretation of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"}
                smooth
                = succeed "-groupoid" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            of the higher Lie algebra become tangents to the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphisms"} k-morphisms
            of this smooth
            = succeed "-groupoid." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            do make sense.
          %p
            On the other hand, it is of course possible to consider the structure on “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            \-algebras without grading”, even if these will not have a good theory. This notion has once been introduced by Filippov (Sib. Math. Zh. No 6 126–140 (195)) under the term
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-Lie+algebra"} n-Lie algebra
            \.
          %p Beware, therefore, that the innocent-looking difference between the terms
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+n-algebra"} Lie n-algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-Lie+algebra"} n-Lie algebra
          %p corresponds, unfortunately, to a major difference in the behaviour of the concepts behind these terms.
          %p
            In conclusion, it is clear that 2-brane physics is governed by Lie 3-algebraic structures, but it is not yet clear how the trinary operation highlighted by
            %a{:href => "#BaggerLambert"} BaggerLambert
            would be an example.
          %p
            In view of this it might be noteworthy that the equivalent reformulation and generalization of the BLG model by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ABJM+model"} ABJM model
            does not involve any “3-algebras” at all. In fact at least most of the “3-algebras” appearing in the membrane literature may be understood as being data of a plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            with an invariant product and a representation (
            %a{:href => "#MFMR"}> MFMR 08
            ). These authors summarize the state of affiars on p. 3 as
          %blockquote
            %p All this prompts one to question whether the 3-algebras appearing in the constructions [13,10,11] play a fundamental role in M-theory or, at least insofar as the effective field theory is concerned, are largely superfluous. The equivalence of [10] and [6] and the abundance of new theories (dual to known M-theory backgrounds) which seem not to involve a 3-algebra might suggest the latter. Nonetheless, given our lack of understanding of how to incorporate in Lie-algebraic terms the expected properties of M-theoretic degrees of freedom, like the entropy scaling laws for M2- and M5-brane condensates, it may be useful to understand the precise relation between the 3-algebras appearing in the recent literature on superconformal Chern–Simons theory and Lie algebras.
          %p
            The article (
            %a{:href => "#MFMR"}> MFMR 08
            ) provides this relation and under this relation the “3-algebraic” BLG model has then been understood as a special case of the ordinary Lie algebraic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ABJM+theory"} ABJM theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BaggerLambert12"} Bagger-Lambert 12
          %p
            It has also been suggested that “3-algebras” are to be interpreted in
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+geometry"} 2-plectic structure
            (
            %a{:href => "#SaemannSzabo"}> Saemann-Szabo
            ).
